
JOINS:
--------
In order to connect two tables, we need a common column key (e.g; order_id)

JOINS – types:
--------------
Inner join,
Left join,
Right join, 
Full join.

1.	If you want to see only matching data in tables – Inner Join,
2.	If you want to see all rows of left table and matching data of right – Left join
3.	If you want to see all rows of right table and matching data of left – Right join
4.	If you want to see all rows of both tables – Full join
5.	If you want to see unmatched data only at one side – left anti join
6.	If you want to see unmatched data both side – full anti join

Inner Join:
------------
Syntax:
SELECT * 
From A
Inner join B
On A.key = B.key;

Example:
select 
c.id, 
c.first_name, 
o.order_id, 
o.sales
from customers as c
inner join orders as o
on c.id=o.customer_id;

Left Join:
-----------
Syntax:
SELECT * 
From A
Left join B
On A.key = B.key;

e.g: Get all customers along with orders, including without ordered.
select 
c.id, 
c.first_name, 
o.order_id, 
o.sales
from customers as c
left join orders as o
on c.id=o.customer_id;

Right join:
------------
Syntax:
SELECT * 
From A
Right join B
On A.key = B.key;
e.g: Get all orders info along with customers, including without matching customers.
 
select 
c.id, 
c.first_name, 
o.order_id, 
o.sales
from customers as c
right join orders as o
on c.id=o.customer_id;

Full Join: 
-----------
Returns all rows from both tables (matching and unmatching)
SELECT * 
From A
full join B
On A.key = B.key;

Example:
select 
c.id, 
c.first_name, 
o.order_id, 
o.sales
from customers as c
full join orders as o
on c.id = o.customer_id

